    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d317">

                <pb facs="1486" n="1459" sameAs="frus:frus1981-88v11/pg1459"/>

                <head>317. National Security Decision Directive 310<anchor n="1" xml:id="d317-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1988-07-18">July 18, 1988</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE TENTH NST
                        NEGOTIATING ROUND</hi> (C)</p>
                <p>The attached instructions provide guidance for the tenth round of the Nuclear and
                    Space Talks (NST), which begins on July 12, 1988, in Geneva. They include the
                    agreements reached during my June 1988 meeting with General Secretary Gorbachev
                    in Moscow. (S)</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Ronald Reagan</hi></signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d317-a1.xml" xpointer="d317-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d317-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d317-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Security Council, National Security Council Institutional Files,
                    Box SR 097, NSDD 310 [NST Negotiations Round X]. Secret. Negroponte distributed
                    the decision directive to Bush, Shultz, Carlucci, Herrington, Webster, Crowe,
                    and Burns under cover of a July 18 memorandum that reads in part: “Agency
                    proposals to limit the scope of technical data exchanged, to specify treaty
                    duration and to define space launch vehicles were deleted from the instructions
                    without prejudice and will be reconsidered as part of the START work program.”
                    (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
